      "text": "To solve \\(\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3}\\), we first need to reduce the angle to an equivalent angle between \\(0\\) and \\(2\\pi\\). This can be done by subtracting multiples of \\(2\\pi\\) from \\(\\frac{11 \\pi}{3}\\) until the angle is within the desired range.\n\nWe start by calculating the number of full rotations (multiples of \\(2\\pi\\)) that fit into \\(\\frac{11 \\pi}{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11 \\pi}{3} = 3 \\pi + \\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(3 \\pi\\) is equivalent to \\(\\pi\\) in terms of radians (because \\(3 \\pi\\) is the same as 1 full rotation), we can simplify \\(\\frac{11 \\pi}{3}\\) to \\(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\).\n\nSo, \\(\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3} = \\sin \\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\).\n\nNext, we need to determine the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\). The angle \\(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\) is in the third quadrant, where the sine function is negative. The reference angle for \\(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4 \\pi}{3} - \\pi = \\frac{4 \\pi}{3} - \\frac{3 \\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe sine of the reference angle \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Since \\(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\) is in the third quadrant, where sine is negative, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{4 \\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
