      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "By using the multiple angle formula for sine, we can rewrite $\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}$ as $\\sin \\left(3\\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right).$\n\nSince $\\sin (3\\pi + \\theta) = -\\sin \\theta,$ we have\n\\[\\sin \\left(3\\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sin \\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right).\\]\n\nFinally, using $\\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\\[-\\sin \\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\]\n\nTherefore, $\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = \\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}.$\n\nWe used the fact that the sine function is periodic with a period of $2\\pi$ to rewrite $\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}$ as $\\sin \\left(3\\pi + \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right).$ Then, we used the multiple angle formula for sine to simplify the expression and find that $\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nThe final answer is \\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}.",
