      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we first need to simplify the angle \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) to an equivalent angle within the interval \\([0, 2\\pi)\\). We do this by subtracting \\(2\\pi\\) (which is \\(\\frac{6\\pi}{3}\\)) from \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) until the resulting angle is within the desired range.\n\nFirst, we calculate:\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}.\n\\]\nThe angle \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is within the interval \\([0, 2\\pi)\\), so we can use this angle directly. Now, we need to find the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nThe angle \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is in the fourth quadrant, where the sine function is negative. The reference angle for \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\\[\n2\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{6\\pi}{3} - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}.\n\\]\nTherefore, \\(\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = -\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\). Since \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}.\n\\]",
